# Part 1: API Selection
# API Name
# PokéAPI
# Description
# PokéAPI is a free public API that provides data about the Pokémon universe, including:

# Pokémon information
# 1-Abilities
# 2-Moves
# 3-Types
# 4-Statistics

# Base URL:
# https://pokeapi.co/api/v2

# Part 2: Postman Configuration
# A new collection was created in Postman called:
# PokéAPI Collection

# Three requests were added.
# Request 1 — GET Pokémon Details
# Method:
# GET
# Endpoint:
# https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/pikachu
# Expected response:
# {
#  "name": "pikachu",
#  "height": 4,
#  "weight": 60
#  }
# Status Code:
# 200 OK
# Request 2 — GET Pokémon List
# Endpoint:
# https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon
# Response includes a list of Pokémon resources.
# Request 3 — Example DELETE Request
# PokéAPI is read-only, so POST/DELETE are not supported.
# Example concept:
# DELETE /pokemon/1

# Part 3: Request Analysis
# Status Codes Observed
# Code	Meaning
# 200	Successful request
# 404	Resource not found
# 500	Server error
# Example Response JSON
# {
#  "name": "pikachu",
#  "height": 4,
#  "types": [
#    {
#      "type": {
#        "name": "electric"
#      }
#    }
#  ]
# }

# Important Headers
# Example:
# Content-Type: application/json
# Cache-Control: public

# Part 4: Technical Explanation
# Request 1
# Method
# GET
# Endpoint
# /pokemon/pikachu
# Description
# Returns detailed information about Pikachu.
# Request 2
# Method
# GET
# Endpoint
# /pokemon
# Description
# Returns a list of Pokémon available in the API.

# What I Learned
# I learned that APIs allow applications to communicate by sending HTTP requests and receiving structured responses such as JSON. APIs make it possible for different systems to exchange data and build modern web services.
